{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 280,
    "dDurationMs": 2680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Raycon for supporting PBS"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2960,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Scientists have been slowly extending the periodic \ntable one element at a time, pushing to higher and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8240,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "higher masses, and have discovered some incredibly \nuseful materials along the way. But the elements  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14680,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the current end of the table are so unstable \nthat they decay almost as soon as they’re created  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20200,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in our particle accelerators. Have we reached the \nend of the line of discoverable elements? There  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27240,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are new rows of the periodic table to unlock, and \nmore stable versions of known heavy elements to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33000,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "synthesize—and while our accelerators are \ncoming up short, astronomers have found a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39960,
    "dDurationMs": 8760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strange cosmic phenomenon that may populate \nthe periodic table beyond our wildest dreams."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48720,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "—-"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52240,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A little while ago we talked about the Island of \nStability—the patch of the periodic table where  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57840,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nuclear physics tells us we may find \nheavy elements of unusual stability,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63080,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and perhaps with unusual and useful properties. \nWe stopped short of talking about how to actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69400,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "make these things. In fact, it turns out it may be \nnext to impossible—at least with current methods  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76320,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of smashing nuclei together. But astrophysicists \nof all people may have figured out a way to create  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82000,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "elements in the island of stability. Instead \nof building a large nucleus from a smaller one,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88080,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how about taking two atomic nuclei the size of \ncities, smash them together, and see what comes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94560,
    "dDurationMs": 7720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out? Sounds impractical, but the universe does \nthis pretty regularly with neutron star mergers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102280,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re going to see how this phenomenon may \nallow us to prove the existence of the Island of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106800,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Stability, and how new techniques in the lab \nare pushing the far end of the periodic table."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we can fix the problem, let’s talk about \nthe problem itself—nuclear instability. So a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117720,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nucleus is basically a balancing act between \nthe repulsive force between protons trying to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124280,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "blow the nucleus apart via the electromagnetic \nforce, and both protons and neutrons sticking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131360,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "together via the strong nuclear force. This is \nwhy neutrons are important: they ensure protons  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137000,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "don’t get so close together that their repulsive \ntendencies overwhelm the attractive strong force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142760,
    "dDurationMs": 189,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For small nuclei, the most energetically \nfavorable arrangements tend to involve  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142949,
    "dDurationMs": 2051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "roughly equal numbers of protons and neutrons. For \nmany of the most common elements in the universe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145000,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the most stable form is when neutron \nnumber equals proton number—like helium-4,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150160,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "carbon-12, oxygen-16, and magnesium-20. By \nthe way, these numbers are atomic mass number,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157280,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and are the total number of nucleons—protons \nplus neutrons—and they designate the isotope  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164880,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the named element. The element is defined \nby the atomic number—the number or protons,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170880,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and each element can have different \nisotopes—different numbers of neutrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175680,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For the lighter elements, neutron and \nprotons numbers are often similar.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180360,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the repulsive electrostatic or “Coulomb \nforce” grows quickly as we add protons. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186360,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "further we go along the periodic table the \nmore neutrons we need as padding between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191040,
    "dDurationMs": 8040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "protons to dampen that force. For example, \niron-56 has 4 more neutrons than protons,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199080,
    "dDurationMs": 9120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gold-179 has 21 more neutrons than protons, \nand lead-208 has a ‘neutron excess’ of 44."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208200,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Beyond a certain total number of nucleons, \nit becomes impossible for the short-range  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214400,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strong nuclear force to hold the nucleus \ntogether against the enormous electrostatic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218920,
    "dDurationMs": 10520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "repulsion. The heaviest truly stable element \nis lead-208, with 82 protons and 126 neutrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229440,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Part of what makes Lead-208 special is that \nit is ‘doubly magic.’ Nucleons in a nucleus  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237680,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are not little balls held together \nby glue like we see in our textbooks,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242080,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they’re fully quantum mechanical objects that \noccupy 'shells' the same way that electron  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247080,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orbitals do. And just as with electron orbitals, \nthese nuclear shells are more stable when properly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253040,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "filled up with “magic” numbers of protons \nand neutrons—as is the case with lead-208.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260720,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Without full nuclear shells, an element \nis more susceptible to radioactive decay."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266920,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But beyond lead-208, even full nuclear shells \ncan’t save these overstuffed nuclei. At some  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274440,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "point, excess repulsive force between \nthe protons causes it to fall apart—in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279480,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the most unstable cases splitting into two \nsmaller nuclei in what we call spontaneous  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285320,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fission. More commonly the decay is \nvia the emission of an alpha particle,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289720,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is the same thing as a helium-4 nucleus \nwith 2 protons and 2 neutrons, or a beta particle,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294840,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is just an electron being spat out to turn \na neutron into a proton. All elements beyond lead  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301360,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are radioactive and eventually decay this way. \nAnd the larger the nucleus, the quicker this is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307560,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "likely to happen. Some of these radioactive \nelements decay slowly enough that we still  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312720,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "find them in Earth’s crust—albeit diminished in \nquantity from when they were forged in supernova  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318840,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "explosions and the like. Other naturally \noccurring radioactive elements are the decay  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324440,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "products of more massive nuclei, or are created \nwhen stable nuclei are hit with cosmic rays."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331560,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But some nuclei are so short-lived that we \nnever find them in nature. These we have to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337880,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "make in labs by smacking more stable nuclei with \nalpha particles or neutrons to build up to higher  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343680,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "atomic numbers. That has allowed us to crack the \n100 mark in number of protons—to what we call the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350240,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "super-heavy elements. But to get all the way up \nto the current heaviest known elements—currently  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357080,
    "dDurationMs": 8520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Oganesson, with atomic number 118—we can’t build \nthem up slowly. That’s because nuclei this large  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365600,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decay quicker than we can add neutrons or alpha \nparticles. Instead, these are made by smacking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371320,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "two smaller nuclei together—for example Oganesson \nwas first made by smashing calcium nuclei into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378200,
    "dDurationMs": 7480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "californium atoms—quite a lot of times to get \neven a barely detectable amount of Oganesson."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385680,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the way, calcium-48 has been a go-to \nfor this sort of element building because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391200,
    "dDurationMs": 8280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it also has doubly-magic filled nuclear shells. \nVery recent work by scientists at Berkeley Lab  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has used titanium-50 instead, which greatly \nincreases the yield of super-heavy elements,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 405240,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the researchers have their eyes set \non the next row of the periodic table,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409640,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in particular the as-yet-unnamed elements 120."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415320,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But these new elements are going to be \nextremely unstable, just as the current  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419680,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "super-heavy’s are. For one \nthing, the versions of these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423480,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "elements that we create with current \nmethods don’t have enough neutrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428520,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Remember that the ideal ratio of neutrons to \nprotons increases with atomic number. That  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433240,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "means smashing two lighter elements together \nwill always give you an isotope of the heavy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 438800,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "element with similar neutron-to-proton ratio \nas the lighter elements that went in. I.e. not  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446600,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough neutrons. We could try bombarding the new \nelement with a beam of neutrons to supplement,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452920,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but it’s very difficult to do that \nbefore the unstable isotope decays."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458240,
    "dDurationMs": 8520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The extra impetus to find that neutron-to-proton \nsweet spot is to hit a new doubly-magic region of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 466760,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the periodic table, similar to lead-208 and \ncalcium-48. That’s this theoretical island  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473760,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of stability, which is thought to exist \nat around atomic number 110-114—elements  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480760,
    "dDurationMs": 10920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from darmstadtium to Flerovium, but for isotopes \nwith around 180 neutrons. The versions we’ve  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 491680,
    "dDurationMs": 8440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "created are at least 10-neutrons short of this and \nhave half-lives between milliseconds and minutes,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500120,
    "dDurationMs": 7880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while the stable isotopes may have half-lives of \nat least hours, and perhaps up to a few years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 508000,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s not yet known whether we can make island of \nstability elements with particle accelerators. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 513880,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there may be another way to at least prove \nthe existence of the island. In the past,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 518640,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when our accelerators haven’t been up to \nthe job we’ve turned to the universe. For  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524320,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "example the most energetic cosmic rays have \nvastly more punch than anything the large  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528880,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hadron collider could produce. Well, it \nturns out there’s one event, phenomenon,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534160,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that can potentially create island of \nstability elements—neutron star mergers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540080,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Neutron stars are basically city-sized \natomic nuclei with the mass of stars.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545640,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They are the end state of the core of a \nmassive star after its explosive death,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551400,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when all that central matter collapses \nunder gravity and is converted to almost  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555480,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pure neutrons at nuclear densities. When \ntwo neutron stars find each other—perhaps  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561600,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because their progenitor stars were \nbinary partners—then they’ll eventually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565520,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spiral together and merge. From Earth we see \nthese first as a wash of gravitational waves,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 572720,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "immediately followed by a brief gamma ray burst, \nand then a longer-lasting kilonova explosion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580200,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps the most important outcome of \nneutron star mergers is that we now  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 583440,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "believe that they are one of the key sources of \nheavy elements in the universe. When they impact,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589040,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "huge quantities of neutron matter material is \nsprayed into surrounding space. Freed from the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595440,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extreme pressure of the neutron star, many \nneutrons decay into protons and electrons,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601480,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "forming light atomic nuclei. But these nuclei \nfind themselves swimming in a thick soup of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 606400,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fast-moving neutrons which can quickly \nbuild the nuclei up via the r-process,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611480,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or rapid neutron capture process. Calculations \nshow that this produces elements all the way  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617200,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the end of the naturally-occuring periodic \ntable. We now believe that neutron star collisions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622880,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are essential to making many of the heavy \nelements we see on earth like gold and uranium."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628760,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As the collided neutron star settles down \ninto either a much larger neutron star,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632760,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or more likely a black hole, the explosion \nof new heavy elements spreads outward s. Lots  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 638760,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of ridiculously radioactive new \nelements will be in that cloud,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643240,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and these will quickly alpha-decay, beta-decay \nor fission their way to more stable nuclei."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 649480,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because of the extreme abundance of neutrons, \nthere’s no reason that elements in the island  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654240,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of stability or even heavier shouldn’t \nbe produced. In fact, if heavier elements  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 660400,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are made we’d expect them to undergo a chain of \nrapid decays until they hit the island, where the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667960,
    "dDurationMs": 8800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decay chain would stall for up to years in that \nsemi-stable region and that might be detectable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678560,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, this is all pretty speculative. Let’s \nget to some actual observations and evidence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685320,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The first neutron star merger was observed in \n2017, simultaneously spotted as a gravitational  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 691160,
    "dDurationMs": 9840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wave signal and as a gamma ray burst. GW170817 was \nthen observed as a kilonova—a long-lasting glow of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701000,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radiation as all the radioactive byproducts \nof the merger decayed. That kilonova gave  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707040,
    "dDurationMs": 5771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "us evidence that neutron star mergers do at \nleast produce some heavy elements, but there’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712811,
    "dDurationMs": 3549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only so much you can determine with a single \nevent. I’ll come back to what these kilonovae  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 716360,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can potentially can tell us as we see more of \nthem—but first some more real evidence. Evidence  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722560,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that elements heavier than uranium—so-called \ntransuranics—are produced in neutron star mergers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729760,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Stars that formed early in the universe \nare expected to have relatively few heavy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734120,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "elements because there hadn’t been many \nprior generations of stars to make those  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738280,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "elements. But such an otherwise unpolluted \nstar that formed shortly after a neutron star  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745200,
    "dDurationMs": 7720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "merger should bear the particular elemental \nsignature of that explosion’s debris. These  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752920,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stars give us our current best picture of what \nexact elements are produced in a neutron star  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 758920,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "merger. Many of these stars are actually \nstill around, orbiting in the Milky Way,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 764640,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and we discover them through the unusual \ncombination of elements seen in their spectra."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769640,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now we can’t directly detect any superheavy \nelements in these stars—remember, these are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775520,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stars formed near the beginning of the universe, \nand so those unstable elements have long-since  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780920,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decayed. However we can look for an excess of the \nstable decay products of these elements—ruthenium,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rhodium, palladium, and silver. And we’ve \nfound cases of the high abundances of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 794600,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these decay products, suggesting that neutron \nstar mergers forged large amounts of elements  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 800760,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like californium—a transuranic that’s a few \nticks up the periodic table from uranium.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 807080,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We haven’t yet pinned down similar evidence \nfor island of stability elements, but perhaps  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 812680,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by finding more of these ancient stars stained \nby neutron star merger we’ll be able to do so."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819920,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s also hope of observing island isotopes \na little more directly. I mentioned that after  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 827600,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a neutron star merger, there’s \nsomething called a kilonova,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 831160,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is a fading afterglow of light from \nthe decay of radioactive nuclei. Well,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836080,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if extremely heavy nuclei decay \nthrough the island of stability,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 840160,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we might expect that to be reflected in the way \nthe kilonova fades. The way a kilonova changes in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847440,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "brightness definitely reflects the half-lives \nof lighter radioactive isotopes—for example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854640,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there’s a brightness bump peaking at around 100 \ndays that’s driven by the decay of isotopes in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 860360,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actinide series that have half-lives in that \nrange. The island of stability elements have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866320,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "half-lives ranging from hours to years, and it’s \nspeculated that the quicker-decaying elements in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 872080,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this range might leave a detectable bump in the \nfirst several hours after the kilanova begins."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879400,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In order to detect this, we’d have to catch the \nkilonova as soon as possible—really right after  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884480,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we spot the gravitational waves from the merger. \nWe’ve only observed one highly resolved kilonova,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 891760,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "again from GW170817, and it took \nastronomers 11 hours to find the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 897080,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electromagnetic source after LIGO caught \nthe gravitational wave signal. By then any  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 903120,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very massive nuclei would have decayed \nalready, so we missed any transition  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 907840,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the rate of fading caused by the island of \nstability—if that transition happened at all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914080,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it’s not clear that these magic island \nelements can even be produced this way. Our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 921160,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nuclear physics tells us that the heaviest \nelements produced in the r-process after  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 926200,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the neutrons run out should have proton \nnumbers of around 100 and neutron numbers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 931040,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of around 200. If a dozen-ish of those \nneutrons then beta-decay into protons,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 938280,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then it’s possible these could wind \nup as island of stability elements."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 943400,
    "dDurationMs": 20160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if we’re lucky, and the nucleus doesn’t \njust fall apart during that chain of beta decay,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 963560,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then neutron star mergers could produce \nenormous numbers of nuclei in the island  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968000,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of stability. We just need to \ncatch a kilonova quickly enough."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 972520,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Usually it’s our labs here on earth that \nhelp us understand what we’re seeing out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 976360,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there in the universe. Perhaps now \nwe astrophysicists can give back a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 981360,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "little by proving that the island of \nstability exists—then our labs can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 985720,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get on with building them. Perhaps we’ll do \nit with the very next neutron star merger,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 992520,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the following cascade of radioactive decay \nshining at us from distant parts of spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 999440,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Raycon for supporting PBS. If you \nwant deep focus when you’re watching Space Time  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1004760,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or doing the math for your world changing theory \nof everything, you probably want to tune out the  "
    } ]
  }, {
